      "page_idx": 17,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.166,
            0.064,
            0.378,
            0.081
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 6"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.119,
            0.884,
            0.209
          ],
          "content": "rectangle marking the points as shown and measure the diagonals. In rectangle PQRS, the right angles at P and R are referred to as opposite angles. The other pair of opposite angles are the right angles at Q and S."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.21,
            0.884,
            0.322
          ],
          "content": "Observe that a diagonal divides each of the pair of opposite angles into two smaller angles. In the figure, the diagonal PR divides angle R into two smaller angles which we simply call g and h. The diagonal also divides angle P into c and d. Are g and h equal? Are c and d equal?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.324,
            0.882,
            0.37
          ],
          "content": "First predict the answers, and then measure the angles. What do you observe? Identify pairs of angles that are equal."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.166,
            0.384,
            0.289,
            0.407
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Explore"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.412,
            0.882,
            0.459
          ],
          "content": "How should the rectangle be constructed so that the diagonal divides the opposite angles into equal parts?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.467,
            0.884,
            0.558
          ],
          "content": "How will you record your observations? First, identify the parameters that need to be tracked. They are the sides of the rectangle and the 8 angles formed by the two diagonals. Are there any other measurements that you would want to keep track of?"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.169,
            0.565,
            0.879,
            0.664
          ],
          "content": "| Sides | A | B | C | D | E | F | G | H |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_17_table_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.679,
            0.884,
            0.748
          ],
          "content": "In your experimentation, did you consider the case when all four sides of the rectangle are equal? That is, did you consider the case of a square? See what happens in this special case!"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.169,
            0.769,
            0.806,
            0.838
          ],
          "content": "What general laws did you observe with respect to the angles and sides? Try to frame and discuss them with your classmates."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.205,
            0.842,
            0.806,
            0.887
          ],
          "content": "How can one be sure if the laws that you have observed will always be true?"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.822,
            0.773,
            0.908,
            0.83
          ],
          "content": "Math Talk I"
        },
        {
